# Standard Reader — environment variables
#
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in real values. `.env` / `.env.*` are gitignored
# (only `.env.example` is committed).
#
# TanStack Start env model: ONLY variables prefixed with `VITE_` are exposed to the
# browser. Everything else is server-only — keep all secrets unprefixed.

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Database — Neon Postgres (read-model / cache)
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Pooled connection string from the Neon dashboard (used by the app + drizzle-kit).
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://[user]:[password]@[neon_hostname]/[dbname]?sslmode=require&channel_binding=require"
# Use the pg Pool driver instead of the Neon HTTP driver. The HTTP driver makes
# one HTTPS fetch per query; the Pool keeps warm TCP connections so sequential
# queries skip per-request TLS/proxy overhead. In prod (Railway ↔ Neon, same
# region) the TCP+TLS handshake is <5ms — far cheaper than per-query HTTP.
DB_DRIVER="pg"
# Request pool size, and how long a query may wait for a free connection before
# it errors. node-postgres queues acquires forever if unset, which turns pool
# exhaustion into multi-minute hangs that still answer 200 — set this so a
# drained pool fails loudly instead of silently.
DB_POOL_MAX="16"
DB_POOL_ACQUIRE_TIMEOUT_MS="5000"
# Separate pool for the OAuth refresh advisory lock, which holds its connection
# across the PDS token-refresh round trip. Isolating it means a slow third-party
# PDS can stall refreshes without starving ordinary signed-in reads.
DB_LOCK_POOL_MAX="8"
DB_LOCK_ACQUIRE_TIMEOUT_MS="20000"

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Analytics — Plausible (client-side, `@plausible-analytics/tracker`)
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Site domain as registered in Plausible. Leave blank to disable tracking.
VITE_PLAUSIBLE_DOMAIN="standard-reader.app"
# Optional: proxy endpoint (see https://plausible.io/docs/proxy/introduction).
# VITE_PLAUSIBLE_ENDPOINT="https://standard-reader.app/api/plausible/event"
# Set to "true" to send events from localhost during dev (default: off).
# VITE_PLAUSIBLE_CAPTURE_ON_LOCALHOST="true"

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Observability — OpenTelemetry → Honeycomb
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Tracing is started by `@opentelemetry/auto-instrumentations-node/register`, which
# the `start` and `ingest:start` scripts load via `--require`. There is no
# bootstrap code — everything below is read by the SDK itself.
#
# Note the register hook is NOT in the `dev` script, so `vite dev` is untouched.
# Where it does load without an endpoint configured, the SDK still records and
# buffers spans, quietly failing to export to the default http://localhost:4318.
# That is harmless but not free; to switch tracing off outright (local ingest
# runs, CI) set:
#   OTEL_SDK_DISABLED=true
# which installs a no-op provider. `src/server/observability/log.ts` keeps
# writing its stdout JSON lines either way.
#
# Traces route to a dataset by OTEL_SERVICE_NAME, not by a dataset header — the
# name below deliberately matches the pre-OTel dataset so history stays in one
# place. Events written before the OTel cutover have no trace_id; filter on
# `trace.trace_id exists` to look at traced traffic alone.
OTEL_SERVICE_NAME="standard-reader"
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT="https://api.honeycomb.io"
# Honeycomb ingest key. Without this header the SDK exports successfully into a
# 401 and you get silence, not an error — if traces go missing, check here first.
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS="x-honeycomb-team=YOUR_INGEST_KEY"
# http/protobuf is the default and the right choice; grpc needs no extra deps here.
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_PROTOCOL="http/protobuf"
# EU instance: https://api.eu1.honeycomb.io
#
# Auto-instrumentation emits a span per outbound DNS/TCP/TLS/HTTP/DB operation,
# so volume runs several times the pre-OTel event count. If that becomes a cost
# problem, prefer tail sampling (Refinery) over head sampling — at low error
# rates, head sampling makes rare failures effectively invisible:
# OTEL_TRACES_SAMPLER="parentbased_traceidratio"
# OTEL_TRACES_SAMPLER_ARG="0.25"

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# App / server
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Public base URL of the deployed app (no trailing slash). The AT Proto OAuth
# client derives its client_id (`${PUBLIC_URL}/api/auth/atproto/metadata.json`),
# jwks_uri, and redirect URI (`${PUBLIC_URL}/api/auth/atproto/callback`) from it.
#
# Local dev: use http://127.0.0.1:3000 — AT Proto treats `localhost`/`127.0.0.1`
# base URLs as a public (loopback) client, so no signing key is needed locally.
PUBLIC_URL="http://127.0.0.1:3000"

# Railway PR previews inherit the production PUBLIC_URL above from shared
# variables, which would send the OAuth round-trip back to prod. On any Railway
# deployment whose RAILWAY_ENVIRONMENT_NAME is NOT the production environment,
# the app instead derives its base URL (and thus the OAuth client_id /
# redirect_uri / jwks_uri) from that deployment's own RAILWAY_PUBLIC_DOMAIN, so
# login returns to the preview. Production must run in an environment named
# "production"; if yours is named differently, set this to that name.
# RAILWAY_PRODUCTION_ENVIRONMENT="production"

# Constellation backlink index — discovers Bluesky posts and margin.at notes linking article URLs.
# Default public instance; override to point at a self-hosted Constellation.
CONSTELLATION_URL="https://constellation.microcosm.blue"

# Slingshot (microcosm) — optional fallback for repo reads (e.g. Sifa `id.sifa.profile.self`).
# SLINGSHOT_URL="https://slingshot.microcosm.blue"

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Auth — AT Proto / Bluesky OAuth (@atcute/oauth-node-client)
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# In production (non-loopback PUBLIC_URL) the app runs as a *confidential* OAuth
# client and must authenticate with a private signing key. Set this to an ES256
# private key as a JWK JSON string (one line). It is served (public half only)
# at `${PUBLIC_URL}/api/auth/atproto/jwks.json`.
#
# Generate one with @atcute/oauth-node-client, e.g.:
#   node -e "import('@atcute/oauth-node-client').then(async m => console.log(JSON.stringify(await m.generateClientAssertionKey('standard-reader-oauth'))))"
# The `kid` argument is REQUIRED: the signed client_assertion advertises it and
# the auth server uses it to find the matching key in the published JWKS. A key
# without a `kid` fails login with "no applicable key found in the JSON Web Key
# Set". (Any ES256 JWK works as long as it has a `kid`.) Leave blank for local
# loopback dev.
ATPROTO_PRIVATE_KEY_JWK=""

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Ingestion — Jetstream v2 consumer service
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The standalone Node worker (`pnpm ingest:dev`) subscribes to Jetstream v2,
# replays the sealed archive from its stored cursor, then cuts over to the live
# tail. There is no separate ingest daemon to run alongside it any more.
#
# Port for the worker's admin routes (`/api/ingest/status`,
# `/api/ingest/recompute`, `/health`).
INGEST_PORT="3099"
# Shared secret the ingestion admin routes require. Generate with:
# openssl rand -hex 32
INGEST_WEBHOOK_SECRET=""
# Jetstream v2 instance. The other public one is jetstream.us-west.bsky.network.
JETSTREAM_SERVICE="https://jetstream.us-east.bsky.network"
# API key for Jetstream's archive endpoints, from https://bsky.network/account.
# The live tail is unauthenticated; only replaying history needs this, so an
# unset key degrades to "live tail only" rather than failing outright.
JETSTREAM_API_KEY=""
# Concurrent archive block downloads during a replay. Throughput plateaus around
# 16–32 against the public instance and concurrency 64 gets nearly every request
# 429'd, so 16 is the safe shoulder.
JETSTREAM_BLOCK_CONCURRENCY="16"
# How many events from one batch may be applied at once. The per-event work is a
# chain of round trips to Neon, so >1 keeps the connection busy instead of idling
# on latency; the ceiling is what stops it owning the whole pg pool
# (`DB_POOL_MAX`, 16 by default).
JETSTREAM_APPLY_CONCURRENCY="16"
# PLC directory used for DID → PDS/handle resolution (blob URLs, profile enrich).
TAP_PLC_URL="https://plc.directory"

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Lexicon publishing — app-owned `app.standard-reader.*` schemas
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The lexicons live in `./lexicons/` and are published as `com.atproto.lexicon.schema`
# records via the `goat` CLI (install: https://github.com/bluesky-social/goat):
#   pnpm lex:lint                  # validate schemas
#   pnpm atproto:publish-lexicons  # upload new/updated schemas to the account below
#
# Credentials for the account that controls the `standard-reader.app` NSID
# authority. Use an app password (Bluesky settings → App Passwords), NOT the main
# password. Publishing also requires `_lexicon.standard-reader.app` DNS TXT
# records resolving to this account's DID — see `goat lex check-dns`.
LEXICON_PUBLISH_IDENTIFIER=""
LEXICON_PUBLISH_APP_PASSWORD=""

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Feedback agent — userinput.app board automation (CI)
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Credentials for the feedback space *owner* (`standard-reader.app`). Only the
# owner's `app.userinput.status` records are honored, so these must be that
# account — not the posting bot (`READER_BOT_*`). Use an app password.
# Set as GitHub Actions secrets for `.github/workflows/feedback-agent-status.yml`.
USERINPUT_OWNER_IDENTIFIER=""
USERINPUT_OWNER_APP_PASSWORD=""
# Optional; defaults to the owner's PDS (stropharia.us-west.host.bsky.network).
# USERINPUT_OWNER_PDS_URL=""

# Claude Code Routine fired for each discussion marked `planned`. Create the
# routine at https://claude.ai/code/routines, add an API trigger, then set both
# as GitHub Actions secrets for `.github/workflows/feedback-agent.yml`.
#
# The token is the routine's OWN token from the API-trigger modal — scoped to
# firing just that routine, and shown exactly once. It is NOT the account-wide
# token from `claude setup-token`. Rotate or revoke it from the same modal.
CLAUDE_ROUTINE_ID=""
CLAUDE_ROUTINE_TOKEN=""

# Trigger: airglow.run (https://airglow.run) watches our org repo
# (did:plc:f4os2wz5fjl56xpwcvtnqu7m) for a newly-created `app.userinput.status`
# record and fires a GitHub `repository_dispatch`, which starts
# `.github/workflows/feedback-agent.yml`. No credentials live in this repo for
# this — configure the automation in airglow with a JSON webhook action:
#
#   Method:  POST
#   URL:     https://api.github.com/repos/hipstersmoothie/standard-reader/dispatches
#   Headers: Authorization:       Bearer {{secret:github_pat}}
#            Accept:              application/vnd.github+json
#            X-GitHub-Api-Version: 2022-11-28
#            Content-Type:        application/json
#   Body:    { "event_type": "feedback-planned" }
#
# The PAT behind {{secret:github_pat}} needs `repo` scope (classic) or
# fine-grained Contents: read-write on this repo — that's what `dispatches`
# authorizes against. Optionally include the record's AT-URI so the poller can
# target just that discussion instead of re-scanning the whole board:
#   { "event_type": "feedback-planned", "client_payload": { "uri": "<at-uri>" } }
# Either way the poller only acts on `planned` discussions, so filtering the
# airglow trigger on state == "planned" is an optimization, not a correctness
# requirement — a dispatch for any other state just reports "0 dispatched".

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Perf regression — Playwright load budgets (local / CI optional)
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Base URL for perf tests (defaults to http://127.0.0.1:3000). Reuses a running
# `pnpm dev` when available (see playwright.config.ts).
# PERF_TEST_BASE_URL="http://127.0.0.1:3000"
#
# Signed-in suite (preferred): Bluesky handle + app password (Settings → App
# Passwords). The perf runner logs in once per run and seeds a dev session.
# Also set PERF_TEST_APP_PASSWORD on the dev server (.env) so it can restore
# the app-password AT Proto client alongside the session cookie.
# PERF_TEST_IDENTIFIER="your.handle.bsky.social"
# PERF_TEST_APP_PASSWORD="xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx"
#
# Optional: override PDS for login (defaults to https://bsky.social).
# PERF_TEST_PDS_URL="https://bsky.social"
#
# Legacy fallback: paste the HttpOnly `standard-reader-auth.session_token` cookie
# after OAuth login (requires a matching OAuth session in the DB).
# PERF_TEST_SESSION_TOKEN=""
#
# Discover stable article/publication/tag fixtures from your DB:
#   pnpm perf:discover-fixtures
# PERF_TEST_ARTICLE_PATH="/a/did:plc:…/rkey"
# PERF_TEST_PUBLICATION_PATH="/p/did:plc:…/rkey"
# PERF_TEST_TAG="observability"
# PERF_TEST_SEARCH_QUERY="reader"
# Collection editor target (signed-in). rkey of a collection doc in the
# signed-in user's repo — pick one with many items. Skipped when unset.
# PERF_TEST_COLLECTION_RKEY="rkey"            # or PERF_TEST_COLLECTION_EDIT_PATH="/collections/edit/rkey"
#
# Scale all time budgets on slow runners (default 1).
# PERF_BUDGET_MULTIPLIER="1.5"
# PERF_TEST_TIMEOUT_MS="60000"
# PERF_TEST_WARMUP_PASSES="1"
#
# View the latest JSON report in a browser:
#   pnpm perf:view   # http://127.0.0.1:3098
#
# The reader guide's screenshots reuse every variable above — base URL, sign-in
# credentials, and the article/publication/tag fixtures:
#   pnpm guide:shots   # writes public/guide/*.png  (see screenshots/README.md)
# Guest screens need none of them; signed-in screens and fixture-backed screens
# skip rather than fail when the matching variable is unset.
# GUIDE_SHOTS_TIMEOUT_MS="45000"

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# API docs — live curl examples (/docs/api)
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Examples auto-discover publication/article/tag fixtures from the DB when unset.
# Override any field explicitly, or reuse PERF_TEST_* vars (see above).
# List endpoints need a real public list on a PDS:
# API_DOCS_FIXTURE_LIST_URI="at://did:plc:…/app.standard-reader.list/…"
# Smoke-test all auto-runnable examples:
#   pnpm api-docs:examples

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Weekly digest email (comail / atmos.email)
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Server-only. The self-contained `digest-cron` Railway service runs the whole
# send (query → build → render → comail) via `pnpm digest:send`, so it needs the
# full set below PLUS DATABASE_URL and PUBLIC_URL (already in this file). The
# `web` service additionally needs DIGEST_UNSUBSCRIBE_SECRET so the one-click
# unsubscribe route can verify tokens. Neither the `ingest` worker nor `web`
# runs the send itself.
#
# Local run: `pnpm digest:send:dev` (loads this .env).
#
# Sending account DID + API key from comail domain enrollment (https://comail.at/docs):
# COMAIL_DID="did:plc:…"
# COMAIL_API_KEY="atmos_…"
# From address on the enrolled sending domain:
# COMAIL_FROM="digest@standard-reader.app"
# HMAC key for signing one-click unsubscribe links (any long random string):
# DIGEST_UNSUBSCRIBE_SECRET=""
#
# Optional tuning:
# COMAIL_SEND_URL="https://smtp.atmos.email/v1/send"  # override for testing
# DIGEST_MAX_PER_RUN="90"      # cap per weekly run (stay under comail's hourly ceiling)
# DIGEST_SEND_DELAY_MS="1500"  # throttle between sends

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Web push notifications (VAPID)
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# "Notify me when this publication/author posts". The tap worker queues a row
# the instant a new document is indexed; the self-contained `push-cron` Railway
# service drains and sends it via `pnpm push:send` every five minutes.
#
# Which service needs what:
#   push-cron  — all three below, PLUS DATABASE_URL and PUBLIC_URL. This is the
#                only process that ever holds the private key.
#   web        — VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY only, so `getPushConfig` can hand the browser
#                the application server key to subscribe with.
#   ingest     — nothing. Its whole involvement is one INSERT.
#
# With the keys unset the cron exits cleanly having done nothing, so a PR
# preview pointed at a shared database can't send real notifications carrying
# preview-domain links.
#
# Generate a pair with: pnpm --filter standard-reader exec web-push generate-vapid-keys
# VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=""
# VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=""
# Contact URL for the push services, `mailto:` or `https:`:
# VAPID_SUBJECT="mailto:hello@standard-reader.app"
#
# Optional tuning:
# PUSH_MAX_DOCUMENTS_PER_RUN="20"  # documents drained per invocation
# PUSH_MAX_SENDS_PER_RUN="2000"    # ceiling on individual notifications per run
# PUSH_SEND_CONCURRENCY="8"        # endpoints pushed to in parallel
#
# Local run: `pnpm push:send:dev` (loads this .env).

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Topic naming (recompute sweep)
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Server-only, needed by the `ingest` service. The sweep clusters the tag
# co-occurrence graph into topics and asks Claude to name each new cluster
# (`src/server/ingest/topic-naming.ts`). Names are cached by cluster
# identity, so a steady-state sweep makes roughly no requests — only the first
# run, and later only genuinely new topics.
#
# Optional: without a key every topic falls back to a deterministic name
# (its most central tag, title-cased), so a local sweep still works.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY="sk-ant-…"
#
# Optional: override the naming model (default `claude-opus-5`).
# TOPIC_NAMING_MODEL="claude-opus-5"
#
# Semantic tag edges. Off unless set to "1". When on, the sweep embeds each tag
# by the titles of articles carrying it and adds edges between tags that mean
# the same thing but never co-occur — without it `typescript` and `css` end up
# in separate topics, because the people who tag one are not the people who
# tag the other. Vectors are cached in `tag_embeddings`, so only the first run
# pays for the model (~4 min for the whole vocabulary; ~40s once warm).
# TOPIC_EMBEDDINGS="1"
#
# Optional: override the embedding model (default `Xenova/all-MiniLM-L6-v2`).
# Changing it re-embeds every tag.
# TOPIC_EMBEDDING_MODEL="Xenova/all-MiniLM-L6-v2"

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Weekly "hottest articles" Bluesky thread (thread-cron)
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Server-only. The self-contained `thread-cron` Railway service posts a weekly
# thread of the network's hottest articles as the reader bot via
# `pnpm thread:post` (Fridays 16:00 UTC), so it needs the app-password creds
# below PLUS DATABASE_URL and PUBLIC_URL (already in this file).
#
# The job posts at most once per ISO week: it claims the week in
# `weekly_thread_runs` before composing, and writes the posts at week-derived
# rkeys via putRecord — so a redeploy, restart, retry, or hand-run on a week
# that already went out does not publish a second thread.
#
# Local run: `pnpm thread:post:dev` (loads this .env). Set THREAD_DRY_RUN=1 to
# compose + log the thread without writing any records or claiming the week.
# Set THREAD_FORCE=1 to deliberately re-post a week that already went out (it
# overwrites that week's thread in place rather than adding a new one).
#
# Posting account app password (falls back to PERF_TEST_* above for local dev):
# READER_BOT_IDENTIFIER="your.handle.bsky.social"
# READER_BOT_APP_PASSWORD="xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx"
# READER_BOT_PDS_URL="https://bsky.social"  # optional, defaults to bsky.social

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# KOReader progress sync (kosync)
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Server-only, `web` service. HMAC key behind each reader's e-reader sync key
# (Settings → E-readers). The key is derived, not stored — so this secret is the
# only thing standing between a guessed DID and someone else's reading position,
# and rotating it invalidates every reader's key at once (they re-copy it from
# Settings). Any long random string; generate with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
#
# In development it falls back to a fixed placeholder so the flow works without
# setup; in production the app refuses to start the sync routes without it.
# KOSYNC_SECRET=""
